<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/periodic-table">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Chemistry, on the foundational track. It assumes no
    prior chemistry — we start from a sorting problem you already understand from
    everyday life. Every new word is explained the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what the periodic table is, and why its pattern repeats;</li>
    <li>read the rows (periods) and columns (groups), and say what each one tells you;</li>
    <li>describe the alkali metals, halogens, noble gases and transition elements in plain words;</li>
    <li>predict a reaction from an element's position — before any experiment;</li>
    <li>explain the trends in reactivity and in atom size.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with a sorting problem</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose someone hands you 63 different metals and powders and gases, all mixed
    up, and asks you to file them sensibly. That was chemistry's problem in 1869.
    In that year, Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the known elements by atomic weight —
    and noticed that their properties <em>repeated</em>. Lithium, sodium and
    potassium were all soft metals that react furiously with water. Fluorine,
    chlorine and bromine were all volatile, corrosive non-metals. The pattern came
    back around, like the months of a year — hence "periodic".
  </p>
  <p>
    Mendeleev did something braver than sorting. Instead of forcing every element
    into the pattern, he left gaps for elements nobody had found yet — and
    predicted what they would be like. When gallium (1875), scandium (1879) and
    germanium (1886) turned up on schedule — germanium matching his "eka-silicon"
    almost exactly — the table became chemistry's foundation. Today it runs to
    <strong>118 elements</strong> (the last four named in 2016), ordered by proton
    count rather than weight.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="electronegativity across the first 20 elements — the saw-tooth resets at every new shell" widget="plotter" config={enCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> hover along the saw-tooth and read the values: the pull
    climbs step by step within each row toward the halogens, then crashes back to a low at
    each alkali metal where a new shell begins. Notice that fluorine, at 3.98, keeps the
    record across all twenty elements plotted. The teeth repeat with the same shape row
    after row — that repetition is the entire meaning of the word periodic.
  </p>
  <p>
    The zig-zag above <em>is</em> the table's logic. It plots
    <strong>electronegativity</strong> — an atom's pull on shared electrons. The
    pull climbs across each row toward the halogens, then crashes back at the
    alkali metals where a new electron shell starts. Fluorine, at 3.98 on the
    Pauling scale, is the hungriest element of all — which is why it attacks even
    glass, and why it took chemists until 1886 to isolate it, at the cost of
    several lives and careers. The shape is set by electron shells filling and
    refilling, one row at a time.
  </p>

  <h2>How the table is arranged</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="how to read the table in four steps" widget="equationStep" config={readCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order and read them as
    a procedure: across a period the same outer shell fills; down a group the outer count
    stays the same; and the third panel is the payoff — same valence, same chemistry. The
    fourth panel is the historical proof: Mendeleev's blank spaces, filled in 1886 by
    germanium matching his prediction almost exactly. Hold the first three steps in mind
    and the whole table reads as two short rules.
  </p>
  <p>
    The modern table orders elements by <strong>proton number</strong> (atomic
    number) — the count of protons in the nucleus, which is the atom's true
    identity — and stacks them so that the outer-electron count repeats
    vertically. The horizontal <strong>periods</strong> (rows 1–7) correspond to
    electron shells: moving left to right, electrons fill the same outer shell (2
    in period 1, then 8, then 8, then 18…), which is why the rows have those
    lengths.
  </p>
  <p>
    The vertical <strong>groups</strong> (columns) collect elements with the same
    number of outer (valence) electrons. Since the outer shell runs the chemistry,
    a group behaves like a family: group 1 metals all explode in water, group 17
    elements all attack, and group 18 gases react with almost nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Roughly 75% of the table is <strong>metals</strong>, sitting left of the bold
    staircase line: shiny, malleable, conductive. Right of the line,
    <strong>non-metals</strong> are dull, brittle when solid, and poor conductors.
    Elements astride the staircase — silicon, germanium — are the semiconductors
    your phone is built from. Reading the table fluently — group tells you the
    outer electrons, period tells you which shell — turns 118 separate facts into
    one system.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> elements repeat their behaviour in a fixed order;
    rows mark filling shells, columns mark families with the same outer
    electrons — and the staircase line splits metals from non-metals.
  </p>

  <h2>Group I: the alkali metals</h2>
  <p>
    Lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium (and radioactive francium) all
    carry a single outer electron, and they would rather do almost anything than
    keep it. That loose electron gives them their physical signature: these metals
    are <em>soft</em> (a knife slices sodium), <em>light</em> (lithium and sodium
    float on water), and low-melting — lithium melts at 181 °C, caesium at just
    28.5 °C, so on a warm day caesium is a liquid metal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drop a pea-sized piece in water and the chemistry is theatrical: it skims the
    surface fizzing, releases hydrogen gas, and forms an alkaline hydroxide —
    2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. Lithium fizzes gently; sodium ignites with a
    yellow-orange flame; potassium ignites instantly with a lilac one; rubidium
    and caesium detonate on contact, shattering the dish. The trend is strict:
    <strong>reactivity increases down the group</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason is pure geometry. Each successive element has one more full shell
    between the nucleus and that outer electron, which shields the electron from
    the nucleus's pull — so it is lost ever more easily. This is also why alkali
    metals are stored under oil (to keep air and moisture off) and why none of
    them occurs free in nature: they live locked inside salts like NaCl.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the heavier the metal, the more reactive" is a decent rule for
  Group I — but students often learn the opposite because for many <em>other</em> metals
  (zinc vs copper, say) reactivity does fall with position. The Group I trend runs the same
  direction as the atomic size: bigger atom, more shielding, looser electron, fiercer reaction.
  And a second one: burning sodium does not make the water alkaline — the <em>hydroxide formed</em>
  does.</p>

  <h2>Group VII: the halogens</h2>
  <p>
    Fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine (and astatine) sit one electron short of a
    full shell — seven outer electrons — which makes them the strongest
    electron-grabbers in existence. They never occur as single atoms: each exists
    as a <strong>diatomic molecule</strong>, meaning two atoms bonded together —
    F₂, Cl₂, Br₂, I₂. Their states at room temperature descend the group from gas
    (fluorine, chlorine) to brooding red-brown liquid (bromine) to grey
    crystalline solid (iodine), and the boiling points behind that progression
    climb steadily:
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="halogen boiling points climb down the group — bigger molecules, stronger mutual attraction" widget="chart" config={halogensCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> hover over the four bars for exact numbers and watch
    them climb down the group: fluorine boils at −188 °C, iodine at 184 °C — a swing of
    more than 370 degrees within one family. Notice where room temperature would cut across
    the chart: fluorine and chlorine sit below it, which is why they are gases at room
    temperature, bromine's bar just clears it into liquid, and iodine's soars past into
    solid territory. The climb tracks molecule size, not bond strength.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rising trend has nothing to do with the strong F–F or Cl–F bonds; those
    are already broken. It is the <em>attraction between whole molecules</em>: more
    electrons per molecule means stronger temporary flickering charges and a
    stickier family. Reactivity, though, runs the <em>opposite</em> way from the
    alkali metals: <strong>it decreases down the group</strong>. Fluorine is
    violently reactive, chlorine fierce, bromine moderate, iodine tame.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason: to react, a halogen must pull an electron <em>in</em>, and as the
    atom gains shells the incoming electron feels an increasingly shielded,
    distant nucleus. A <strong>displacement reaction</strong> — one element
    pushing another out of its compound — shows the pecking order directly. Bubble
    chlorine gas through colourless potassium bromide solution and the solution
    turns orange: chlorine has stolen the electron and displaced bromine
    (Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂), but bromine cannot displace chloride. Only the
    hungrier element wins.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the halogen trend is <em>not</em> the Group I trend. Alkali
  metals get more reactive down the group (losing an electron gets easier); halogens get less
  reactive down the group (gaining one gets harder). The two families bracket the table's
  appetite like a pair of jaws — group 1 giving electrons away, group 17 taking them.</p>

  <h2>Group VIII (0): the noble gases</h2>
  <p>
    Helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon sit at the table's right edge
    with <em>full</em> outer shells — helium's pair, the rest octets. With no
    electron to gain or lose, they have no motive: they are colourless, odourless
    <strong>monatomic</strong> (single-atom) gases that form essentially no
    compounds. That aloofness is their industrial virtue. Argon blankets molten
    steel and fills light bulbs because the hot filament cannot burn it; helium —
    far lighter than air and, unlike hydrogen, completely non-flammable — lifts
    balloons and cools MRI magnets; neon's reluctance to react is what makes neon
    signs possible, since a glowing, unreactive gas just keeps glowing. Radon,
    radioactive and heavy, is instead a hazard, seeping from granite into
    basements.
  </p>

  <h2>The transition elements</h2>
  <p>
    The squat block in the middle — iron, copper, zinc, chromium and friends —
    plays by its own rules. Unlike the alkali metals, transition elements are
    <strong>hard, dense, and high-melting</strong> (iron melts at 1,538 °C;
    tungsten at 3,422 °C, which is why it made the best filaments — though
    mercury, oddly, is liquid at room temperature). Their chemistry superpower is
    having several possible charges in their compounds, which shows up in names:
    iron can be iron(II) or iron(III), copper copper(I) or copper(II), each
    forming different compounds. Their ions are famously <strong>coloured</strong>
    — copper(II) blue, iron(II) pale green, iron(III) yellow-brown, permanganate
    deep purple — and their ability to hand electrons back and forth makes them
    superb <strong>catalysts</strong>, substances that speed up reactions without
    being used up: iron in the Haber process that makes fertiliser, nickel in
    margarine hydrogenation, platinum in your car's catalytic converter. Modern
    civilisation is disproportionately built on the middle of this table.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> group 1 gives electrons away ever more eagerly down
    the column; group 7 grabs them ever less eagerly; group 8 wants no electrons
    at all; and the middle block plays by its own hard, colourful, catalytic
    rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the table to make predictions</h2>
  <p>
    The table's real power is that it lets you <em>predict before you test</em> —
    the skill the exam actually rewards. Take two questions you should be able to
    answer without a laboratory. First: potassium sits directly under sodium in
    group 1, so it must have one outer electron like sodium's — but with two extra
    shells shielding it. Prediction: it reacts with water like sodium, only faster
    and hotter (it ignites with a lilac flame and skips across the surface).
    Second: chlorine and bromine both sit in group 7, bromine further down.
    Prediction: chlorine is the more reactive (its incoming electron feels less
    shielding), so chlorine displaces bromine from its salts but not the reverse.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same logic extends to formulas. An <strong>ion</strong> is an atom that
    has lost or gained electrons, so it carries a charge. Knowing barium is in
    group 2 (two outer electrons to give away, Ba²⁺) and chlorine in group 7 (one
    to gain, Cl⁻) lets you write BaCl₂ before any experiment; knowing oxygen is
    group 6 (O²⁻) gives BaO.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trends also march in <em>size</em>. Atoms shrink as you cross a period —
    the nucleus gains protons that pull every electron harder while the new
    electrons pile into the same shell — and jump larger down a group, as whole
    new shells stack on. This single radius trend explains, in one move, why
    ionisation energy rises across a period and falls down a group, why metallic
    character (the ease of losing electrons) increases toward the bottom-left, and
    why the most greedy non-metals sit at the top-right — the diagonal that
    electronegativity's saw-tooth in fig. 1 traces. When two arguments collide,
    chemists say the element sits "on the diagonal": silicon and germanium, metal
    and non-metal at once, are the working proof.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> hydrogen's lonely position at the top of group 1 does not
  make it an alkali metal. It shares the single-outer-electron address but is a reactive
  <em>non-metal</em> gas that forms H⁺ — which is why some tables give it a column of its own
  (or three positions at once). Its placement is an argument, not a fact; the behaviour, not
  the column, is the chemistry.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Sodium reacts more fiercely with water than lithium does. Why, in terms of shells and shielding?</li>
    <li>Chlorine and iodine are both halogens. Which is more reactive, and what would chlorine do to potassium iodide solution?</li>
    <li>Aluminium sits in group 3 (Al³⁺) and oxygen in group 6 (O²⁻). Write the formula of their compound.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Sodium has one more full shell than lithium
    shielding its outer electron, so that electron is lost more easily — and
    losing it is what drives the reaction. (2) Chlorine is more reactive; it
    would displace iodine, turning the colourless solution brown. (3) Al₂O₃ —
    two lots of 3+ (six positive) balance three lots of 2− (six negative), so
    the charges cancel.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Elements are ordered by proton number, and properties repeat because outer electron shells fill and refill.</li>
    <li>A period fills one shell; a group shares one outer-electron count — and the outer shell runs the chemistry.</li>
    <li>Group 1 alkali metals get more reactive down the group, as the outer electron is ever more shielded from the nucleus.</li>
    <li>Group 7 halogens get less reactive down the group as electron-grabbing weakens — the opposite trend, bracketing the table's appetite.</li>
    <li>Group 8 noble gases have full outer shells and form essentially no compounds.</li>
    <li>The staircase line splits metals (about 75% of the table) from non-metals, with the semiconductors astride it.</li>
    <li>Position predicts reactions before any experiment: chlorine displaces bromine, and ion charges write formulas like BaCl₂ for you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The patterns you have just read off the table are consequences of the atom itself:
    atomic structure opens the nucleus and shells that make each column behave as a family,
    and the mole and stoichiometry lesson turns the table's atomic masses into a working
    tool. From there, acids, bases and pH puts whole columns into action — the reactive
    hydroxides, the acidic oxides.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Mendeleev's table had one quiet scandal: a few elements sat in the wrong order by weight —
    tellurium (127.6) before iodine (126.9) — which he fixed by trusting properties over mass and
    simply swapping them. He was right, but nobody could say <em>why</em> until 1913, when Henry
    Moseley measured the X-ray wavelengths of many elements and found they lined up with a
    single integer: the proton number. The table's true alphabet is the nucleus, not the weight,
    and Moseley's law even flagged the missing elements left as gaps — several of which he
    correctly predicted. He was killed at Gallipoli in 1915 at 27; scientists have wondered since
    what else he would have found.
  </p>
  <p>
    The table's bottom rows hide further architecture. The lanthanides and actinides are pulled
    out and parked below to keep the table printable, but they belong inside periods 6 and 7 —
    which is why the f-block is sometimes called chemistry's cellar. And the hunt for element 119
    continues at heavy-ion accelerators, where atoms are forged a nucleus at a time and survive
    for milliseconds. Theory predicts an "island of stability" of superheavy isotopes with
    unusually long lives; meanwhile, oganesson (element 118) is forecast to be a <em>reactive
    solid</em>, not a noble gas at all — the full outer shell stops protecting you once
    relativity distorts the inner electrons. Even the table's most dependable family may end in
    an exception.
  </p>
  <p>
    The families are also hidden infrastructure. Caesium's group-1 chemistry — one electron,
    flipped between two energy states by microwave light — is so precisely repeatable that the
    second itself is <em>defined</em> as 9,192,631,770 caesium oscillations; every GPS fix,
    stock trade and internet clock is disciplined by an alkali metal's electron. Gallium, a
    post-transition metal that melts in your palm (30 °C) and sits near the staircase, forms the
    compound semiconductors (GaAs, GaN) inside phone radios and the blue LEDs that won a Nobel
    Prize in 2014. Helium, the noble gas discovered in the Sun's spectrum (1868) before it was
    found on Earth, cools MRI magnets and quantum computers — and, being light enough to escape
    the atmosphere entirely, is the table's only element that regularly leaks off the planet.
    The columns are not just a filing system; they are the periodic table's supply-chain map.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mendeleev (1869); Moseley, "The High-Frequency Spectra of the Elements" (1913); IUPAC periodic table (118 elements); Pauling electronegativity scale; Cambridge IGCSE Chemistry 0625 syllabus (2026–2028), section 8. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
